# Ornith 1.5 35B-A3B on a 24 GB card
#
# Restore with:
#     cp docs/profiles/ornith15-35b.env .env
#     $EDITOR .env                          # MODELS_DIR is machine-specific
#     ./scripts/serve.sh up
#
# Every value here was measured on an RTX 3090 (udesa) on 2026-08-20, across
# five benchmark batches with a thermal control run in each. EXPERIMENTS.md
# section 11 has the tables and the negative results.
#
# READ THIS FIRST IF YOU ARE ADAPTING ANOTHER PROFILE: three of the defaults
# this repo carries are WRONG for this model, and each one fails silently
# rather than loudly. They are marked below.

# --- Where the .gguf files live on the host ---
MODELS_DIR=/home/nromero/models

# --- Model ---
# ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-GGUF -- 21,713,462,848 B (20.2 GiB), MIT.
# Architecture is qwen35moe: a mixture of experts, 256 experts with 8 active,
# so roughly 3B parameters run per token. That single fact drives most of the
# decisions in this file -- see SPEC_TYPE especially.
#
# Q4_K_M is not a preference, it is the only quant that fits. Q5_K_M is
# 23.6 GiB of weights against a 24,576 MiB card, leaving nothing for the KV
# cache. Quantization is therefore a constraint here, not a tuning knob.
MODEL_FILE=Ornith-1.5-35B-Q4_K_M.gguf
MODEL_ALIAS=ornith15-35b
CONTAINER_NAME=ornith15
PORT=8080

# --- Memory ---
# 262144 is the model's own declared maximum, and it fits: 23,830 MiB of
# 24,576, measured. That leaves only 746 MiB free, which is fine on a
# DEDICATED card and is not fine on a shared one. If anything else may want
# this GPU, drop to CTX=131072 with KV_TYPE=f16 (23,046 MiB, 1,530 free) and
# lose 3% of decode.
#
# Raising the context costs VRAM and nothing else. Measured decode rate at
# q8_0 across the range: 160.4 t/s at 65k, 161.5 at 131k, 161.6 at 196k,
# 162.6 at 262k -- flat inside the noise, with a thermal control run
# confirming the card does not drift.
#
# The KV cost is 13.6 KiB/token measured, against the 11.7 KiB/token that
# scripts/gguf-info.py computes from the header. The script undershoots by 17%
# because it models the cache and not the context-dependent compute buffers.
# It undershot the 9B by 29%. Always leave room for the gap.
CTX=262144
NGL=all

# CAREFUL -- this is the value that contradicts .env.example, which claims
# q8_0 costs "half of what f16 does, at negligible loss".
#
# On THIS model f16 is not merely lossless, it is FASTER: 168.0 t/s against
# 160.4 at 65k, and 167.7 against 161.5 at 131k. Consistent, ~4%. The reason
# is that the model barely has a KV cache to begin with -- 11 of 41 blocks
# carry one, at head_count_kv 2 -- so the bandwidth q8_0 saves is small, while
# the dequantization it adds to every attention op is not.
#
# f16 is chosen against anyway, and only because it does not fit at this
# context: f16 costs 21.0 KiB/token and OOMs above 131,072. The trade taken
# here is 3% of decode for 2x the context. Reverse it by setting f16 and
# CTX=131072 if your prompts never approach 131k.
#
# DO NOT set KV_TYPE_K and KV_TYPE_V to different types. A more precise K cache
# with a cheaper V looks like the obvious middle ground and is a trap: the flash
# attention kernel has no path for mismatched K/V and falls back silently.
# Measured at CTX=262144 -- q8_0/q8_0 gives 161.4 t/s, q4_0/q4_0 gives 160.4,
# and the mixed q8_0/q4_0 gives 58.9 with prefill down from 3,252 to 244 t/s.
# The penalty is the mismatch itself, not the quantization level. There is no
# warning in the log; the server loads and answers correctly. Section 11.4.
KV_TYPE=q8_0

# --- Sampling: read from the GGUF, not from the model card ---
# The file carries general.sampling.temp=1.0 / top_k=20 / top_p=0.95 in its
# own metadata. The model card's Python examples say temperature=0.6, but
# every benchmark reported in that same card was run at 1.0, and the embedded
# metadata agrees with the benchmarks rather than with the examples. Trust the
# file.
TEMP=1.0
TOP_P=0.95
TOP_K=20
MIN_P=0.0

REASONING_FORMAT=deepseek

# --- Throughput ---
# 27 GiB of system RAM on this host; check `free -g` before raising.
CACHE_RAM=10240
BATCH=2048
UBATCH=512

# CAREFUL -- this is the second value that contradicts the repo's defaults.
# docker-compose.yml defaults SPEC_TYPE to draft-mtp, and EXPERIMENTS.md
# section 1 measures +31% decode from it. That result is real, and it is for a
# DENSE model. It does not transfer here.
#
# This .gguf does ship an MTP head (blk.40.nextn.*), it drafts well, and it
# still loses badly:
#
#     none                    160.4 t/s
#     draft-mtp  n_max=8      122.0 t/s   acceptance 87% (173/198)
#     draft-mtp  n_max=3      129.5 t/s   acceptance 88% (142/162)
#     ngram-simple            158.0 t/s   acceptance 15% (37/240)
#
# Note that acceptance is excellent in both MTP runs -- the drafter is not the
# problem, and no amount of tuning n_max or p_min will fix it. Speculation
# pays when a token from the target model is expensive. In a MoE with ~3B
# active parameters it is cheap, so verification never amortizes. It also
# costs 1.3 GB of VRAM, which at CTX=262144 is VRAM that does not exist.
#
# The general rule this establishes: judge draft-mtp by ACTIVE parameters,
# not by file size. A 20 GiB MoE behaves like a small model here.
SPEC_TYPE=none
SPEC_N_MAX=8
SPEC_P_MIN=0.7

# The address Docker publishes the port on.
#   127.0.0.1  -> this machine only (the default)
#   <tailscale IP, from `tailscale ip -4`> -> reachable from the whole tailnet
#
# NOTE: on the host this profile runs on, a `tailscale serve` proxies
# 127.0.0.1:8080 to https://<node>.<tailnet>.ts.net/. Swapping the container
# behind that proxy changes which model the whole tailnet sees, under the same
# URL and with no authentication. Check `curl https://<node>.../v1/models`
# after any swap. (`tailscale status` prints your own names; they are kept out
# of the repo on purpose — CI rejects a real one.)
BIND=127.0.0.1
